=== Part III evidence: rollback when the candidate is unhealthy ===
captured: 2026-09-28T09:52:42Z

A deliberately broken image (entrypoint exits 3 immediately) is deployed
while a healthy production container is serving. deploy.sh must refuse to
promote it and leave production untouched.

############ PRODUCTION BEFORE ############

$ curl -s http://localhost:8888/version
12-325b7f85
$ docker ps --filter name=devops-hello
NAMES               IMAGE                      STATUS
devops-hello-demo   devops-hello:1.0.0         Up About a minute
devops-hello        devops-hello:12-325b7f85   Up 2 minutes

############ THE FAILED DEPLOY ############

$ ./scripts/deploy.sh devops-hello:broken
deploy: starting candidate devops-hello-cand from devops-hello:broken
eff48b0a3c7db5555439cd67dec2d3aa3f0be42c1ff3f87371f6a18e3c9b1429
deploy: candidate never became healthy at localhost:8889 (last code=0000), rolling back

exit code: 1   (non-zero => Jenkins marks the Deploy stage FAILED)

############ PRODUCTION AFTER ############

$ curl -s http://localhost:8888/version
12-325b7f85
$ docker ps --filter name=devops-hello
NAMES               IMAGE                      STATUS
devops-hello-demo   devops-hello:1.0.0         Up About a minute
devops-hello        devops-hello:12-325b7f85   Up 3 minutes

leftover candidate container? (should be none)
  none - candidate was cleaned up

############ WHAT THIS PROVES ############

1. deploy.sh started the broken image as a candidate and health-checked it.
2. The candidate never returned 200 on /health, so it was removed.
3. The previous production container was never touched and kept serving.
4. deploy.sh exited non-zero, so the Jenkins Deploy stage fails and every
   later stage is skipped - a bad build cannot reach production.
